---
const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Does robots.txt guarantee compliance?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "No. It is a request that well-behaved crawlers honor. For agents that ignore it, enforce at the WAF or edge level instead." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Where must robots.txt be served from?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "The root of every hostname: https://yoursite.com/robots.txt. A file anywhere else is ignored." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "What happens with an empty robots.txt?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Crawlers assume everything is allowed. That is a valid posture; the file only matters when you want per-crawler or per-path rules." } }
  ],
};
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

---
<BaseLayout title="robots.txt: The Complete Guide With Examples" description="robots.txt explained: syntax, user-agent groups, Disallow vs Allow, crawl-delay, sitemap directives, and the mistakes that deindex sites."  
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["AI Web Infrastructure", "/ai-web-infrastructure/"], ["robots.txt Guide"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/ai-web-infrastructure/" class="hover:text-accent">AI Web Infrastructure</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">robots.txt Guide</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">robots.txt: The Complete Guide</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      robots.txt is the first file crawlers request on every site: a plain-text rulebook saying which
      user-agents may fetch which paths. It cannot force anything, but every major search and AI
      crawler respects it, which makes it the single highest-use file in technical SEO.
    </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Core directives</h2>
    <section class="not-prose my-4" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
          <thead><tr><th>Directive</th><th>Example</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">User-agent</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">User-agent: GPTBot</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Target crawler</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Disallow</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Disallow: /private/</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Blocked paths</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Allow</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Allow: /public/</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Exceptions</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Sitemap</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Sitemap: https://...</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Index location</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#anatomy">Anatomy</a></li>
        <li><a href="#the-rules-behind-the-rules">The rules behind the rules</a></li>
        <li><a href="#mistakes-that-cost-real-traffic">Mistakes that cost real traffic</a></li>
        <li><a href="#check-yours">Check yours</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
      <pre class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">User-agent: Googlebot
Disallow: /admin/
Allow: /admin/public/

User-agent: *
Disallow: /cart/
Disallow: /search

Sitemap: https://example.com/sitemap.xml</pre>
      <p>
        Rules group by user-agent: consecutive <code>User-agent</code> lines share the block that
        follows. <code>*</code> matches any bot without its own group. <code>Disallow</code> blocks a
        path prefix; an empty value disallows nothing. <code>Allow</code> carves exceptions inside
        disallowed trees. <code>Sitemap</code> lines can appear anywhere and should.
      </p>
      <h2 id="the-rules-behind-the-rules">The rules behind the rules</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>It lives at the root, exactly <code>/robots.txt</code>, one per host, served as text/plain.</li>
        <li>Most-specific group wins: a named bot ignores the wildcard group entirely.</li>
        <li>Path matching is prefix-based; <code>/blog</code> also blocks <code>/blogroll</code>. Use trailing slashes deliberately.</li>
        <li>Case matters for paths, not for directives.</li>
        <li>Some crawlers stop reading after 500 KB.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="mistakes-that-cost-real-traffic">Mistakes that cost real traffic</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><code>Disallow: /</code> shipped from staging: the classic site-wide deindex.</li>
        <li>Blocking CSS/JS paths that pages need to render, degrading how search engines see the page.</li>
        <li>Expecting robots.txt to hide content: it only prevents crawling, not indexing of linked URLs; use noindex or auth for that.</li>
        <li>AI-era oversight: wildcard blocks written years ago now exclude GPTBot, ClaudeBot, and PerplexityBot too.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="check-yours">Check yours</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="/robots-txt-checker/">robots.txt checker</a> validates syntax line by line and
        shows AI-bot verdicts; the <a href="/ai-crawler-checker/">AI crawler checker</a> goes deeper
        per bot.
      </p>
      
<h2 id="related">Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/ai-crawlers-list/">The complete AI crawler list</a></li>
        <li><a href="/how-to-block-ai-crawlers/">Blocking AI crawlers</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does robots.txt guarantee compliance?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No. It is a request that well-behaved crawlers honor. For agents that ignore it, enforce at the WAF or edge level instead.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Where must robots.txt be served from?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The root of every hostname: https://yoursite.com/robots.txt. A file anywhere else is ignored.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What happens with an empty robots.txt?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Crawlers assume everything is allowed. That is a valid posture; the file only matters when you want per-crawler or per-path rules.</p></details></div>
      </div>
</BaseLayout>
